WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.600
Astronomers became fascinated with mars&nbsp;
4,000 years ago when they first charted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.760
its movements in the sky. But it wasn't until&nbsp;
1965 that we got a good look at its surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:19.760
When NASA sent the Mariner 4 on a flyby, it&nbsp;
captured these images and our imagination. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.440
look at the Mars missions that we've done in the&nbsp;
past, one builds upon the other. NASA is building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.560
toward the dream of sending people to the red&nbsp;
planet. The agency's new spacecraft system wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.480
to get astronauts there by 2030 and it's not the&nbsp;
only one. China is developing bigger and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.600
rockets for that purpose and SpaceX wants to build&nbsp;
a city on Mars with a million inhabitants by 2050.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.760
We're gonna send people to mars and make&nbsp;
life multi-planetary. We've heard so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.080
about the plans to go to Mars but what&nbsp;
will it actually feel like to live there?

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.880
A year on Mars is almost twice as long as a&nbsp;
year on Earth, lasting 687 days - that's how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.760
long it takes to orbit the sun because it sits&nbsp;
farther away. There are also four seasons but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.000
they'll feel different. Mars is very very cold&nbsp;
- not only because of the distance from the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
but due to the atmosphere being 100 times thinner&nbsp;
than Earth's so it doesn't retain heat very long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.160
The average temperature is minus 80 degrees&nbsp;
Fahrenheit. It can get as cold as -195 °F in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.240
winter near the poles or as warm as 70 °F during&nbsp;
summer near the equator but that summer day can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.840
plummet at night to -100 °F. A reason for the&nbsp;
extreme temperature swings is that the planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.760
is covered with dust, causing the sand and rocks&nbsp;
to lose their heat quickly. Powerful dust storms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.600
sometimes last weeks to months, even blanketing&nbsp;
regions the size of the U.S. A GPS system could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.200
track these intense storms and other weather&nbsp;
patterns via a martian internet service. SpaceX's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.080
Starlink satellites orbiting earth could be the&nbsp;
forerunner to a similar system circling the red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.600
planet. So the dust and the freezing temperatures&nbsp;
are no doubt two big concerns. There is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.680
one: radiation. Earth's atmosphere and magnetic&nbsp;
shielding protect us from most of the radiation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.160
the universe but outside our cocoon is an entire&nbsp;
world teeming with dangerous particles and rays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.680
And mars thin atmosphere exposes it to much higher&nbsp;
levels of radiation than on Earth. Building homes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.560
underground might make the most sense but they&nbsp;
could be built above land with a protective layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.640
made up of regolith - loose deposits like dust&nbsp;
and broken rocks. There is plenty of that around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.520
You can walk around without a spacesuit indoors&nbsp;
but outside is a different matter. Without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.160
oxygen the spacesuit supplies, you wouldn't be&nbsp;
able to breathe because the air is mainly made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.800
up of carbon dioxide. NASA is experimenting with&nbsp;
turning CO2 into oxygen. Its Perseverance rover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.920
which is expected to land on Mars in February,&nbsp;
is testing this out with an oxygen generator the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.360
size of a car battery. As much as the gas is a&nbsp;
problem for us, it's good for plants - they need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.800
it in the same way we need oxygen. The extremely&nbsp;
cold conditions mean plants would need to grow in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.560
an enclosed structure or indoors. Having enough&nbsp;
food supply is one thing but the key ingredient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:19.600
to make living on Mars possible is water. There&nbsp;
are no large standing bodies of liquid water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.120
although evidence suggests it used to exist.&nbsp;
Plenty of frozen water has been found such as on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.200
the surface of the Korolev crater in the north&nbsp;
pole, measuring 51 miles across. Vast glaciers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.800
are also hidden under blankets of rocky debris&nbsp;
plus water can also be extracted from the soil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.480
The hard part is figuring out how to mine water in&nbsp;
large enough quantities to support civilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.040
Living on Mars will not be easy. There is still&nbsp;
so much to learn to try to make this possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.640
Whether you want to visit as a tourist or&nbsp;
if you plan on moving there permanently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.480
there is a lot to see on the red planet. Natural&nbsp;
wonders to soak in include the largest volcano in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.240
the solar system, Olympus Mons. It's so&nbsp;
big it fits roughly the area of France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.440
Volcanoes tend to be much larger than those on&nbsp;
Earth because Mars has a weaker gravitational pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.520
that allows them to grow taller but less gravity&nbsp;
means it will be harder to walk around. Gravity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:20.240
only about 38% that of Earth's, so if you weighed&nbsp;
100 pounds on Earth you would only weigh 38 pounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.440
on Mars. Studies have shown that the ideal&nbsp;
walking speed is about half your current rate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.160
And after a long day of sightseeing, watching&nbsp;
the sunset will be a unique experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.200
The sun will be smaller due to the distance&nbsp;
and the sky will appear blue because the fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.320
dust allows the blue light to penetrate the&nbsp;
atmosphere more efficiently. When night comes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.320
look out for the two moons: Deimos and Phobos.&nbsp;
They are irregular in shape and unlike our moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.760
So much about the fourth planet is&nbsp;
different than ours but there's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.680
of terraforming Mars - re-engineering it to make&nbsp;
the environment in atmospheres similar to Earth's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.000
-although researchers disagree over whether&nbsp;
this is possible with current technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.120
One proposal is warming up the planet by&nbsp;
vaporizing its ice caps which Musk has suggested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.320
SpaceX even started selling these t-shirts.&nbsp;
Another possibility he's mentioned is using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.240
thousands of solar reflector satellites or giant&nbsp;
mirrors to reflect sunlight on the surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.080
The financial feasibility of this process is&nbsp;
being questioned. Life in its current form will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.400
be far from the comforts of Earth: it's a rocky,&nbsp;
dusty, desert world. Despite the challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.680
living on Mars would feel like something out of&nbsp;
science fiction. If space agencies and companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:41.920
can achieve the monumental task of getting people&nbsp;
and cargo there, this sci-fi dream could become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.960
a reality. Hey guys, I appreciate you watching.&nbsp;
I'm Cindy Pom. So, would you ever want to move to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.120
Mars or at least visit the red planet? Let me know&nbsp;
your thoughts, you can leave them in the comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

